WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Uh-oh.

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:02.310
I just got an email
that our app is down.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.430
Apparently, the
database is hanging,

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:04.920
and we're losing transactions.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.910
MATT DAY: If we set
up a circuit breaker,

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.420
the app can respond to the
error, rather than hanging.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.530
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But
then each of our services

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.747
has to implement its own
logic for circuit breaking?

00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:14.580
MATT DAY: This is an
infrastructure problem.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.490
Why don't we solve it
with Kubernetes and Istio?

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.290
Let me show you.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.146
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.680
So here's my website.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.960
As you can see, it's a
one-stop shop for everything

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.700
you need to buy in
order to be the best

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.930
hipster that you can be.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.660
It's built on Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.060
with the optional
Istio add-on enabled.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.830
One of the neat
things Istio gives

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.290
is visibility into
everything that's going on.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.660
In this case, I'm going to
use the open source Kiali

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.370
console to show that.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.655
As you can see, I have
two microservices--

00:00:48.655 --> 00:00:50.780
my front end, which is
talking to Cloud Memorystore

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.100
for session caching,
and my product catalog,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.470
which is talking to Cloud
SQL for product information,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.430
pricing, and that kind of thing.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.550
Istio has a concept of versions.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.890
And I have two versions
of my product catalog--

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.538
the working version
and the broken version.

00:01:04.538 --> 00:01:06.080
The broken version
simulates the kind

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.090
of issues I've seen in
the past, where service

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.770
takes too long to respond.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.750
This could be due to a
stressed out database

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.150
with millions of transactions
waiting to be processed.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.790
Perhaps there's infrastructure
issues or networking problems.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.410
Whatever it is, it's things that
make my entire website fail.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.330
I can tell you, if I
went to a website that

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.660
took 15 plus seconds to render
a web page every time I clicked

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.790
a link, I'd probably go to
one of their competitors.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.570
And that's exactly what my
broken service simulates.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.570
It takes a connection,
holds it for 15 seconds,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:36.762
and eventually replies.

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.720
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So even
if a service is down,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.595
we can have our application
handle the error,

00:01:40.595 --> 00:01:41.863
rather than just hanging?

00:01:41.863 --> 00:01:42.530
MATT DAY: Right.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.020
With Istio, you could enforce
a blackout, rather than

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.488
a brownout, when the
service is misbehaving.

00:01:47.488 --> 00:01:50.030
In the past, you'd have had to
include all sorts of libraries

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:50.540
to do that.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.452
And it can get
really complicated.

00:01:52.452 --> 00:01:53.660
Let me show you how to do it.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.460
So let's turn on
that broken version,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.380
send some traffic to it,
and show how Istio can help.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.610
One of the core concepts in
Istio is a virtual service.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.470
A virtual service
defines where my traffic

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.120
is routed within the mesh.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.060
So I'm going to say,
send 100% of my traffic

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.330
to the broken version
of my microservice.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.630
If I apply that change and go
back to my app and refresh it,

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.000
you'll see Chrome is
hanging because my back end

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.380
service is hanging.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.780
At this point, you've
lost me as a customer.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.180
But worse, if this
was a payment service,

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.310
you may have even
lost a sales record.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.530
I've seen all of this
and worse in the past

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.480
due to these kind of bugs.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.400
So what can we do
to mitigate it?

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.190
In the past, we might have
used circuit breaker libraries.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.107
The problem with this
approach is that they're

00:02:36.107 --> 00:02:37.380
very language-specific.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.505
So if one app is written
in Java and another in Go,

00:02:39.505 --> 00:02:40.880
they'll have
different libraries,

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.860
and sharing state between
them is difficult.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.290
Istio gives me a way to solve
this with circuit breaker

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.220
patterns.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.300
There are two cool
things at play here.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.210
The first is my
destination rule.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.980
The destination rule
defines not only

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.680
the versions of my application,
but also characteristics

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.360
on how they receive traffic.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.760
In this case, I'm
saying allow a maximum

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.710
of 200 pending requests.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.308
But I could also put things
like TCP session information

00:03:03.308 --> 00:03:04.850
and all kinds of
settings for my mesh

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.558
to understand what
this app can deal with

00:03:06.558 --> 00:03:08.060
and what its tolerances are.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.210
In this case, I can never have
more than 200 pending requests.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.300
The 201st request will
simply see my app is down.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.520
A blackout, not a brownout.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.327
The second thing I can do
is modify my virtual service

00:03:19.327 --> 00:03:21.410
to change the maximum
amount of time a request can

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.060
wait for my app to reply.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.520
By default, this could
be virtually infinite.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.440
But for some apps,
that's not appropriate.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.150
In the case of my
catalog service,

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.820
I never want to wait for
more than one second.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.950
If I apply this
change now, Istio,

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.020
with that maximum
of one second, will

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.330
intercept the call, which
is taking 15 seconds,

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:41.350
and say no, and instead
give me a 503 gateway

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:44.030
error to my app, which I
can deal with very easily,

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.388
and I can just show that
my catalog is offline.

00:03:46.388 --> 00:03:47.930
The cool thing about
this is I've not

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.702
changed a single line of code.

00:03:49.702 --> 00:03:51.410
And the very neat
thing is in my console,

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.780
I have visibility of
these broken requests.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.570
I can see my service is down
without waiting for my users

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:57.770
to go to a competitor.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:58.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Great.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.692
So now our application is able
to properly handle the errors.

00:04:00.692 --> 00:04:02.108
MATT DAY: Right,
and you only need

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:03.530
to change a little
bit of config.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.450
Didn't have to touch
any of your code.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.867
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Don't forget
to subscribe for more great

00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:09.770
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:13.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

